Dear developers and maintainers,

I hit the following KASAN wild-memory-access in the OCFS2 local quota path.
The bug is still reproducible on the current mainline.

Tested kernel:

  840ef6c78e6a ("Merge tag 'nfs-for-7.2-1' of
git://git.linux-nfs.org/projects/anna/linux-nfs")
  Linux 7.1.0-11240-g840ef6c78e6a #28 SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC

The original crash was seen on:

  Linux 7.1.0-rc7-00056-gacb7500801e9-dirty #42 PREEMPT(full)

The reproducer mounts a crafted OCFS2 image and then creates a file on it.
This reaches olq_alloc_dquot(), where OCFS2 writes to the local quota
chunk bitmap:

  ocfs2_set_bit_unaligned(*offset, dchunk->dqc_bitmap);

On the tested mainline kernel, the reproducer triggers:

  BUG: KASAN: wild-memory-access in olq_alloc_dquot+0x8d/0xf0
  Kernel panic - not syncing: KASAN: panic_on_warn set ...

Build/run:

  gcc -pthread -static -O2 repro.c -o repro
  ./repro

